package formal

namespace formal:
    environment situation, proof checker

    section "Formulas and what satisfies them":
        [FORMULA-TYPE] A formula is a statement.
        [FORMULA-TRUTH] Each formula has a truth value in each situation.
        [TRUTH-VALUES] The truth value of a formula is true or false.
        [ATOM-TYPE] An atom is a formula.
        [ATOM-PART] No atom has a formula as a part.
        [FORMULA-SATISFACTION] A situation satisfies a formula exactly when the formula is true in the situation.
        [CONJUNCTION-TYPE] A conjunction is a formula.
        [CONJUNCTION-TRUTH] A conjunction is true exactly when each part of the conjunction is true.
        [DISJUNCTION-TYPE] A disjunction is a formula.
        [DISJUNCTION-TRUTH] A disjunction is true exactly when at least one part of the disjunction is true.
        [NEGATION-TYPE] A negation is a formula.
        [NEGATION-TRUTH] A negation is true exactly when the part of the negation is false.
        [IMPLICATION-TYPE] An implication is a formula.
        [IMPLICATION-PREMISE] An implication has exactly one premise.
        [IMPLICATION-CONCLUSION] An implication has exactly one conclusion.
        [IMPLICATION-TRUTH] An implication is false exactly when the premise of the implication is true and the conclusion of the implication is false.
        [SET-TYPE] A formula set is a collection.
        [SET-MEMBER] Each member of a formula set is a formula.
        [SET-SAT] A formula set is satisfiable exactly when at least one situation satisfies each formula of the formula set.
        [SET-UNSAT] A formula set is unsatisfiable exactly when no situation satisfies each formula of the formula set.
        [MODEL-TYPE] A model is a situation.
        [MODEL-SAT] While a model exists, the model satisfies each formula of the formula set.

    section "Contracts":
        [CONTRACT-TYPE] A contract is a pair.
        [CONTRACT-ASSUMPTION] A contract has exactly one assumption.
        [CONTRACT-GUARANTEE] A contract has exactly one guarantee.
        [ASSUMPTION-TYPE] The assumption of the contract is a formula.
        [ASSUMPTION-SUPPOSE] While a contract exists, the contract supposes the assumption.
        [GUARANTEE-TYPE] The guarantee of the contract is a formula.
        [GUARANTEE-REQUIRE] While a contract exists, the contract requires the guarantee.
        [CONTRACT-SAT] A situation satisfies a contract exactly when the situation does not satisfy the assumption of the contract or the situation satisfies the guarantee of the contract.
        [CONTRACT-VACUOUS] A contract holds vacuously in a situation exactly when the situation does not satisfy the assumption of the contract.

    section "Proofs":
        [RULE-TYPE] An inference rule is a rule.
        [RULE-DERIVES] While an inference rule exists, the inference rule derives a formula from formulas.
        [DERIVATION-TYPE] A derivation is a sequence.
        [DERIVATION-STEPS] Each step of a derivation is an application of the inference rule.
        [CONTRADICTION-TYPE] A contradiction is a formula.
        [CONTRADICTION-SAT] No situation satisfies the contradiction.
        [PROOF-TYPE] A proof is a derivation.
        [PROOF-ACCEPT] While a proof exists, the proof checker accepts the proof.
        [CHECKER-TYPE] A proof checker is a program.
        [CHECKER-STEPS] While a proof checker exists, the proof checker checks each step of the derivation against the inference rules.
        [CALCULUS-TYPE] A calculus is a set.
        [CALCULUS-MEMBER] Each member of a calculus is an inference rule.
        [CALCULUS-SOUND] A calculus is sound exactly when each formula set that yields a contradiction in the calculus is unsatisfiable.
        [CALCULUS-COMPLETE] A calculus is refutationally complete exactly when each unsatisfiable formula set yields a contradiction in the calculus.
        [CALCULUS-TERMINATING] A calculus is terminating exactly when each derivation of the calculus ends.
        [CALCULUS-CONFLUENT] A calculus is confluent exactly when each order of rule applications reaches the same result.
